            {
               "text": "### Images from Guatemala Antropologia\n\nThese images are copyright for Guatemala Antropologia and allowed to be used according to the license determined by them.\n\nTo visualize all images, use the left and right arrows on the slide below.",
               "source": null,
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "markdown",
               "PostMention": ""
            },
            {
               "text": "<p><div class=\"htSlides\"><div class=\"htSlide\"><div class=\"htSlideCounter\">1 / 1</div><img class=\"imgGameSize\" id=\"imgEstela\" onclick=\"htImageZoom('imgEstela', '0%')\"><div class=\"htSlideCaption\">Stele in the Museo de Antropología de Ciudad de Guatemala, Guatemala. Photo taken on <htdate0> (<htcite0>).</div><div class=\"htSlideRefs\"><b>Related content:</b><ul><li>And the missing numbers? - The Etrusco-Romans also used a decimal system, but how did they do it with only 7 numbers? (<htcite1>)</li><li>Texts like vectors - In this content, we will explore how vectors can help us better understand the information and structures present in texts. (<htcite2>)</li><li>Playing with Consonants and Vowels - An interactive game to understand how syllables are created. (<htcite3>)</li><li>Multiplying the Findings - an educational game in which the reader visualizes multiplication while exploring different archaeological sites. (<htcite4>)</li><li>Sequence game (Part I) - In this first stage, learning is reinforced by completing number sequences that go up to the hundreds, helping to consolidate the understanding of basic foundations. (<htcite5>)</li><li>Sequence game (Part II) - Continuing the challenge, this stage presents sequences that extend up to the billions, deepening the mastery of numerical patterns. (<htcite6>)</li></ul></div></div><i class=\"fa-solid fa-chevron-left htSlidePrev\" onclick=\"htPlusDivs(-1);\"></i><i class=\"fa-solid fa-chevron-right htSlideNext\" onclick=\"htPlusDivs(1);\"></i></div></p>",
